 ## Subtopics
 
-### Min-Max Octree Rendering
-An alternative approach that precomputes a min-max octree to accelerate rendering of unclassified volumes, allowing flexible transfer functions but at a slower speed than classified rendering [S1].
-
-### Raw Volume Rendering
-Renders the raw voxel array directly, allowing dynamic transfer functions and octree acceleration, but is slower than classified rendering [S3].
+### [Image-Centric Volume Rendering with SN-SVM Classification](7509-image-centric-volume-rendering-with-sn-svm-classification.md)
+This method enables direct volume rendering by allowing users to interact with raw 2D volume slices to define training data for an SN-SVM classifier, which then automatically classifies all voxels and assigns colors via color harmonization. It distinguishes itself from Classified Volume Rendering by avoiding pre-processing steps such as branch decomposition or contour tree computation, thereby preserving raw data granularity and preventing information loss during initial semantic realization.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Precomputation is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, taking several minutes for large volumes [S1]. Rendering is $O(P \cdot R)$ where $P$ is pixels and $R$ is ray length, but significantly faster than raw rendering due to optimized data access and lack of per-sample transfer function evaluation [S1, S3].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the raw volume and the preclassified volume. The run-length encoding helps compress the classified data [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fastest rendering mode when the classification is fixed; allows rapid iteration over view transformations and shading parameters [S1, S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Inflexible; cannot change the opacity transfer function without re-precomputing the entire volume [S3]. Precomputation time is high [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When many renderings of the same volume are required with the same classification [S1, S3].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the classification needs to be adjusted frequently or interactively [S1].
+*   **Speed:** This is the fastest rendering mode available in VolPack [S3]. It eliminates the need for per-ray classification and octree traversal during rendering [S1, S3].
+*   **Memory:** Requires additional memory to store the preclassified volume structure, which involves run-length encoding [S1].
+*   **Flexibility:** Low flexibility regarding the transfer function. It is only suitable when the classification (opacity transfer function) remains fixed across multiple renderings [S1, S3]. If the transfer function changes, the slower [Direct Volume Rendering](19-direct-volume-ray-casting.md) with min-max octree acceleration should be used [S1, S3].
+*   **Accuracy:** May mask anatomical details or pathology if not carefully configured (e.g., lack of bone subtraction) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Classified Volume Rendering is part of the VolPack library, a software package for volume rendering [S1]. It was designed to provide a high-performance rendering option for scenarios where the volume data and classification are static [S1]. The method relies on the principle that classification is often the most computationally expensive part of the ray casting pipeline when done per-sample, and precomputing it allows the rendering loop to focus solely on projection and shading [S1][S2, unofficial].
+Classified Volume Rendering is part of the VolPack library, a standard software package for volume rendering [S1]. It was designed to address the computational cost of applying transfer functions during ray casting by moving this operation to a preprocessing stage [S1]. The method is particularly relevant in medical imaging, where large volumes (CT/MR) are rendered repeatedly from different viewpoints with consistent tissue classification [S2, unofficial].
 
